+## X2 T1 (2026-10-09): exec-equivalent job control for `run`
+
+Task: TASK.md S1-S5. `heimdall-proto run -- cmd` must be indistinguishable from plain exec of `cmd` under a job-control shell (bash, zsh) and under a non-shell parent, on darwin-arm64 and linux-arm64. Only `internal/run` changed; the Windows path is untouched and still compiles.
+
+### Defects found (observed on the base build, plain exec as the control)
+
+| Scenario | Base behaviour | Cause |
+|---|---|---|
+| S1 non-shell parent, stdio `/dev/null`, a controlling terminal present (modeExclusive), third-party SIGSTOP/SIGTSTP of the child, then SIGCONT to the child | darwin SIGSTOP: exit 255 and the child gone before its SIGCONT (inferred: killed by the orphaned-group SIGHUP). darwin SIGTSTP and Linux both: heimdall-proto stopped itself and never exited (child left a zombie) | every child stop was mirrored by stopping heimdall-proto, although no shell was watching; nobody continues heimdall-proto |
+| S2/S4/S5 SIGSTOP of the child (third party, self, nested bash `suspend`) | darwin: exit 255 at once (no stop notice, meta `exit_code: -1`). Linux: stop reported as SIGTSTP (`$?` 148, not 147) | darwin: Go's BSD `syscall.WaitStatus.Stopped()` is false for a SIGSTOP stop (it takes the BSD WIFCONTINUED encoding for a SIGSTOP stop), so the loop fell through to "exited" with `ExitStatus() == -1`. Both: SIGSTOP was deliberately mirrored as SIGTSTP |
+
+S3 (self-stop, then SIGCONT to heimdall-proto's PID) behaved like plain exec on the base build in the scenario driver; it is kept and now has a regression test.
+
+### Mechanism now (internal/run/run_unix.go, `supervisor`)
+
+- **Wait status decoding** (`decodeWait`): from the status bits, identical on Linux and the BSDs. `syscall.WaitStatus` is no longer used for stops.
+- **Mirror with the child's signal**: a stop is reported by `kill(getpid(), sig)` with the child's own stop signal, SIGSTOP included, so the shell's notice and `$?` (128+sig) match plain exec. heimdall-proto's PID only, never its group (K6a). SIGTTOU stays mirrored as SIGTSTP (heimdall-proto ignores SIGTTOU to call tcsetpgrp from the background).
+- **Mirror only when a job-control shell can be watching**: the terminal foreground is heimdall-proto's or the child's group, or a stdio descriptor is the controlling terminal. Otherwise (S1) the stop is left to whoever sent it, as with plain exec, and heimdall-proto keeps waiting.
+- **Every SIGCONT heimdall-proto receives continues the child** (`resume`): with plain exec that SIGCONT would have reached the child. The child gets the terminal first if heimdall-proto's group holds the foreground (`fg`), never after `bg`. A child that has already exited (zombie) is left to the wait loop.
+- **Stale stop reports** (Q18): a stop report is acted on only if the child is still stopped when it is handled (`childState`: `/proc/<pid>/stat` on Linux, `sysctl kern.proc.pid` `p_stat` on darwin).
+- **The mutex is no longer held while heimdall-proto is stopped** (round-3b gap 4): the wait loop waits for the `resume` that the SIGCONT handler performs, bounded by `stopWait` (1 s) when the stop was not taken; a `stopping` flag keeps the 50 ms foreground poll from handing the terminal back in between.
+- **Orphaned group**: no self-stop. A terminal stop of the child is undone (the kernel would have discarded it with plain exec); a SIGSTOP is left for its sender.
+
+### Remaining deviations and limits
+
+- **S2 (b)**, permitted by TASK.md: after a third party stops the child and continues only the child, heimdall-proto stays stopped (a stopped process cannot see the child continue). The shell lists the job as stopped until `fg`, which ends it with the child's status. The child runs to completion meanwhile and is a zombie until then.
+- A SIGTTOU stop of the child is reported as SIGTSTP (`$?` 128+SIGTSTP, bash "Stopped" instead of "Stopped (tty output)"). Not part of S1-S5.
+- The S1 rule is a heuristic: a non-shell parent that hands heimdall-proto's group the terminal foreground, or gives it the terminal on stdio, is treated like a shell (the stop is mirrored; nobody continues heimdall-proto unless the parent does).
+- Unchanged: an explicit SIGTSTP/SIGSTOP to heimdall-proto's PID stops only heimdall-proto (§9.4 recorded item); SIGCONT forwarding happens only in modeExclusive (in modeShared the child shares heimdall-proto's group, so a group SIGCONT already reaches it).
+- No independent review of this change was available in this task.
+
+### Tests
+
+- `internal/run/jobctl_test.go`: `TestDecodeWait` (table, including the BSD SIGSTOP and both WIFCONTINUED encodings), `TestDecodeWaitRealStop` (a real SIGSTOP stop, continue, death and reaping, decoded and checked with `childState`).
+- `cmd/heimdall-proto/jobctl_test.go`: a minimal job-control shell (`hp-jc-shell`: session leader on a fresh pty, job in its own foreground group, WUNTRACED, takes the terminal back on a stop, runs `fg`/`bg` on request) records the wait statuses a real shell bases its notice and `$?` on. Tests: S2 for SIGSTOP and SIGTSTP; S3; S5 with self and third-party SIGSTOP and `--meta-file`; Ctrl-Z, `bg` (SIGTTIN on the background read), `fg`; S1 with a non-shell parent (`HP_JC_NOJC=1`) for SIGSTOP and SIGTSTP. Against the base `internal/run` code they fail on darwin (`exited 255`; S1 heimdall-proto state `""`/`T`) and on Linux (`stopped SIGTSTP` for SIGSTOP; S1 heimdall-proto state `T`); with the change they pass, also with `-count=10` on both platforms.
+- Real-shell scenario runs (bash and zsh for S2-S5, SIGSTOP and SIGTSTP for S1-S2, both S5 triggers, S1 with and without a controlling terminal and with `/dev/null` or pipe stdio), plain exec as the control, darwin host and the linux-arm64 harness container: 20/20 heimdall-proto runs per platform per pass meet S1-S5, and the stop notices equal the control's.
